hi there Steve Kaufman here and today I  want to talk about what holds people  back from learning languages last night  we had some guests here I have my cousin  visiting from Argentina which is where  she lives but she has a very good  girlfriend here in Vancouver and they  came over for dinner and this girlfriend  of hers said that you know she's now I  guess in her early 50s and she really  wants to learn French and Spanish and  she says a lot of her friends also want  to learn the language and travel in  those countries and of course many  people say this and many people never  take the necessary steps to make it  happen and so my question is like what  holds these people back and in the  conversation I had with this lady last  night you know it was obvious to me that  that she's held that backed by a lot of  unfounded fears and misconceptions she  said first of all you know well I'm old  now I find that I'm forgetting things  I'm she's 15 years younger than I am I  would imagine I forget things I have a  poor memory I have trouble with  pronunciation I don't know all kinds of  reasons why you know and and most people  when they go when they decide they want  to learn the language they decide they  have to go to a class well it takes a  lot of money time commitment to go to a  class typically it's just not two  minutes away you've got to drive to it  or take public transportation you're  sitting in a classroom with other people  which is fun which is social and I think  to that extent going into a class can be  a pleasant social thing to do if if your  goal is to meet people then then that's  a great thing to do but if the goal is  to learn the language you don't need to  go to a class you know I've been at my  check now for a couple of months and I'm  reading the newspaper with difficulty if  I had started check-in a class after two  months I'd be nowhere  I wouldn't be very far along not as far  along as I am and it would have meant a  disruption to my schedule I don't have  the time to go in the evenings or even  during the day and sit in the classroom  and learning Czech so it you know it  reminded me you know Anna and I happen  to receive a copy of this book written  by cattle mom who's a very famous  Hungarian polyglot linguist as I prefer  to call him who spoke sixteen languages  and her method of learning was very much  through input reading and listening she  didn't have the benefit of the Internet  but she had she lived in in communist  Hungary for most of the period when she  was learning languages so she had  limited access to material but whenever  she got her hands on a book or something  she would read it now she probably has  more determination and motivation than  the average person but the fact remains  that we can all learn through input and  capital mom makes the case that that  it's not a matter of talent it's a  matter of hard work one other thing you  know I'm kind of talking about some of  the obstacles some of the misconceptions  that people have  I don't think language learning is hard  work I don't think it's unpleasant work  it needn't be it takes a lot of time but  it can be done in a way that's pleasant  and relatively painless and I always  prefer the easy painless way that's why  for example when I get a book for  learning a language unlike Catalan I  don't do the exercises because I find  that hard work I just listen and read  and eventually things stick and it just  you know if only people would realize  that by by listening to and reading  content of interest and building up  their comprehension their language  skills will improve and people say well  I need someone to talk to what you're  sure you do eventually but you can't  talk to someone if you don't understand  what they're saying so the first step is  to build up your comprehension and that  again gets back to this input based  learning  listening and reading and you talk when  you have the opportunity and the better  you understand and the more words you  have the better you'll be able to take  advantage of the opportunity when the  opportunity presents itself I would just  note it down some of the other things  that people say yeah one of the other  sort of mix about language learning is  that you first have the pep to sort of  get down the fundamentals I think  someone came out beyond my youtube  channel and so first you have to have  the fundamentals you know that's one  thing that I simply don't believe  because the fundamentals you're  constantly acquiring the fundamentals  you're constantly learning and  forgetting certain things that in fact  are perhaps quite fundamental you don't  grasp them initially you don't grasp  them till much much later so that this  it's a very gradual process you it's not  like fundamentals like like building  blocks that you learn the tenses and you  learn the cases there's a Russian or  tenses in French and then that's a  building block upon which you build you  know more and more knowledge until  eventually you have this big structure  which is your capability of French it's  not like that at all you will continue  to make mistakes in tenses in French you  will continue to get mistakes and make  mistakes in masculine-feminine just as I  hear Chinese speakers continue to make  mistakes with he obscene and they speak  English you will continue to make the  most basic fundamental mistakes for a  long long time no matter how much it's  drilled into you and gradually if you  keep at it and if you enjoy what you're  doing and you know again the Trinity if  you're motivated you spend the time and  you start to notice if you begin to  become a little attentive to the  language all these things gradually  you'll get better so it's a process of  gradual improvement rather than one of  having nailing down some fundamentals  and building other fundamentals on top  of you'll always be making the most  fundamental mistakes for a long long  time if you're not prepared to do that  and you shouldn't learn but if you're  prepared to enjoy the pleasure of  communicating in another language  understanding other people in their  language understanding movies understand  songs enjoying exploring and other  culture and language if you're content  to do all those things and still make  the odd mistake when you speak then  language learning is for you if you  think you have to nail down something  right away you're gonna be very  disappointed and you won't make much  progress in my opinion  so what other notes that I have I can't  remember but my advice to people and now  there must be millions of people in  their 50s who have a little more time  who would like to travel who would like  to explore either virtually by learning  a language or physically by first  learning a language and then going to  the country where the language is spoken  just do it just start and take the easy  route it's the easy way around it's not  it's not instant because language  learning takes time but it's not hard  it doesn't require some amazing talent  that it requires perseverance a lot of  listening and reading day after day  after day and building up your  vocabulary so I mentioned this because I  think there are a lot of myths out there  about what you need to do to learn a  language the kind of skill or talent  that you have to have in order to learn  a lot of language the core sort of steps  that you have to follow and I personally  don't believe that I think that if you  commit yourself to a program of daily  listening and reading anyone like I've  done with check anyone that is prepared  to spend the time that I have spent  listening and reading to a language that  the time that I spent on check after two  months they will achieve a lot  guaranteed okay so thank you and I look  forward to your comments
 

